  "commit": "2867ebf3bb8a9f37b92c93e9438cbdf8de08b75d",
  "description": "Written after its first merge rather than opened before it, which is the practice run/0020's closing note recovered: the history witness anchors on the newest run's last mergeSha, so an empty run is unrepresentable, and LOOP.md step 8 already said \"write docs/ledger/runs/NNNN.jsonld for the iteration\" -- a file that DESCRIBES an iteration, merges included.\n\nOPENING STATE, recorded so a later reader can check drift against something rather than against memory. main is 9939c6b, verified green per check name (tests 537, ledger 533, pages 27). No tags exist. No pull requests are open. The v1 GAP IS SIX: item/release-v0-1-0, item/docs-site, item/image-size, item/readme-owner, item/v1-audit, item/tag-v1-0-0. The gap is what this run reports, per decision/0105; the ratio may accompany it and never replaces it.\n\nNOTHING THE LOOP CAN ADVANCE. All three eligible items end at a decision only the owner can make, and each edge was probed rather than assumed: item/docs-site and item/release-v0-1-0 both turned out to have buildable halves, which shipped (PRs #274, #282, #286, #287); item/image-size was probed and has none -- image-size.sh's --print-only shares every measurement line with the gating call tests.yml already runs on every push, and release.yml's PULLED_TAG handoff between its two steps is correct as written. That third result is a finding, not a shrug: it is the first edge in this project that came back empty.\n\nFIVE DECISIONS WAIT ON THE OWNER.\n  1. Enable GitHub Pages (Settings -> Pages -> Source: GitHub Actions, then the DOCS_SITE_PAGES_ENABLED repository variable). Closes most of item/docs-site.\n  2. Authorise a v0.1.0-rc1 tag. Worth cutting now in a way it was not this morning: #282 made changelog-section.sh find a pre-release's notes, #286 made `latest` publish on a non-pre-release tag, and #287 built the three proofs the rc exists to produce. One rc would now count.\n  3. decision/0102's accounting question -- four groups of shipped surfaces the ledger does not track.\n  4. Whether `release` should be gated on the new boot jobs. A GitHub Release is the user-visible announcement, and announcing a version whose arm64 image did not start is worse than the image merely having been pushed; but the existing split was chosen deliberately, so changing it is the owner's.\n  5. Issue #289 -- about.version has no definition in context.jsonld, which blocks item/tag-v1-0-0 clause (4). Carried here because it was briefly held in a run/0021 description that then had to be deleted, and a note that lives only in a deleted file is a note that is gone.\n\nWHAT THIS RUN INHERITS AS METHOD, from the four corrections run/0020 closed on: report the gap rather than the ratio; an item born Completed says so in its own clause; both forms of validate-ledger run locally before any push, because only running the one with MAIN_SHA is how a tree CI refused was called sound; and a reassuring answer to a narrow question does not end an investigation (decision/0104).\n\nTOUCHED IS EMPTY ON PURPOSE, not by omission. This run's one merge is its own ledger PR; no v1 item moved. Listing item/release-v0-1-0 and item/tag-v1-0-0 there because the owner decisions below name them would have been the ratio mistake wearing a different field: a run that touched nothing reporting two items touched. If the owner unblocks one, it gets added when the work lands, not before.\n\nSECOND MERGE, 2f949c8 (#290). pullRequest null like the first: #290 is a ledger-branch PR, and the code it carried implements no item. It carried code because writing this run turned a test red. MainPushMergesRulesTest's recordedShasOfNewestRun() read the newest run unconditionally and asserted, in a comment, that 'the newest run always has more' than one merge; a run written for its first merge has exactly one, and a one-element list has no interior for a hole to be interior to. `guards` failed the same way through mutation/ledger-history-reports-unrecorded-merge, which reported it precisely -- the test 'did not pass with the guard in place, so it cannot prove anything about removing it'. The assumption was never true, only never tested at a run boundary: runs 0018, 0019 and 0020 were each created with merges = [], and the test was added at 1f5f6f8 when run/0020 already held 14. The assumption gave way, not the practice, because requiring two merges would mean a run could never be written at all. Verified by running LedgerValidator directly -- it is framework-free, so it needs no vendor/ -- against the real ledger: the old helper yields 1 sha and trips the precondition exactly as CI did, the new one yields 82 and passes all three legs.\n\nAND ONE THING THE LOOP COULD ADVANCE AFTER ALL, which this run's opening state said did not exist. item/release-v0-1-0's doneWhen still ended 'STILL NOT BUILT, and deliberately: the amd64/arm64 boot jobs this clause assigns to release.yml' -- twenty-four hours after PR #287 put all three of them on main. The correction was written into #287's merge commit message and the PR node's name, but not into the clause, which is the text the next run consults. Amended here, keeping the old paragraph rather than overwriting it, and recorded as decision/0106. The opening state was not wrong about the three eligible items ending at an owner decision; it was wrong that this left nothing to do.\n\nTHIRD MERGE, 26fadac (#291), main verified green per check name at it: tests 542, ledger 538, pages 32. That PR amended item/release-v0-1-0 and wrote decision/0106; this pass applies the same rule to item/tag-v1-0-0, which decision/0106 named in `affects` and which had never been amended since the consultation that wrote it, though #286 and #287 each implement part of it. Doing it in the pass that follows rather than filing it is the rule behaving as intended; a decision that prescribes an amendment and then does not make one is the same defect one level up.\n\nFOUR MORE MERGES, and the run's `touched` is no longer empty: item/tag-v1-0-0 clause (3) and item/readme-owner clause 1 were both discharged, by PRs #295 and #296. Neither item changed status -- each has a remaining clause that needs the tag or the published image -- which is the point decision/0107 records: a clause moved, the gap did not, and both are true at once.\n\n64d10e4 (#292) and 995cbc8 (#293) carry pullRequest: null for DIFFERENT reasons, and the second one is worth more than the convention it looks like. #292 is a ledger-branch PR, the usual case. #293 is not: it fixed CHANGELOG.md, which release.yml turns verbatim into a GitHub Release body, and no item names that surface -- so a PullRequest node for it would need an `implements` entry that does not exist, since the validator requires each one to resolve to an Action and all 26 existing nodes have a non-empty list. That is decision/0102's accounting question arriving as a concrete missing row rather than as an argument: a merged PR on the release critical path that the ledger cannot name.\n\nWHAT THIS PASS CHECKED BEFORE WRITING ITSELF, per decision/0107's own rule. Every open issue is accounted for: #294 and #289 are owner decisions, #115 and #109 were disposed with comments this run, #36 tracks post-v1, and #27/#30/#32/#33/#34/#35 are the six v1 items' own issues. The three eligible items' clauses were re-read individually; what remains in each needs a tag, a Pages setting or a published image. decision/0102's (a)-(d) were re-examined and deliberately left, for the reason set out in decision/0107.\n\nTWO MORE MERGES, both ledger-branch PRs and both pullRequest: null. #297 recorded four merges and wrote decision/0107; #298 corrected LOOP.md step 4 and wrote decision/0108, which found that 0107 had itself been recorded in breach of LOOP.md step 7 -- a decision requiring a change to that file changes it in the same commit or is not recorded yet, a rule carried since decision/0084 and now broken three times.\n\nTHE SWEEP CAME BACK EMPTY THIS PASS, and saying so is the point of decision/0107's rule rather than a formality. No tag exists, no repository variable has appeared, no issue has been answered, and main moved only by this loop's own two governance merges -- neither of which touches any v1 item's clauses. So every clause state is identical to the sweep recorded above, and re-deriving it would produce the same answer from the same inputs, which is not evidence of anything. The two merges recorded here are owed bookkeeping under item/ledger-main-push-record, not a reason the pass exists.\n\nTWENTY-ONE TICKS PASSED WITH NO CHANGE, and then the sweep found something it had been walking past. 46a235b (#299) is recorded here -- it was the trailing gap the validator permits, deliberately left because recording it alone would be a treadmill, and it now rides along with real work as that reasoning said it would.\n\nTHE SWEEP THAT FOUND IT was the same one the preceding ticks ran, with one difference: the open issue list was read as issues rather than as a list of things already accounted for. #109 and #115 had both been DISPOSED on 2026-09-20 -- folded onto clauses of item/api-content and item/api-presigned-upload, which is LOOP.md step 1's second option -- and every tick since read 'disposed' and moved on. Both items then completed and both issues stayed open, because nothing in the loop closes an issue that is not an item's own. The preceding ticks' sweeps were not wrong about the clauses; they never asked whether the ISSUES were still live.\n\n#115 IS CLOSED, correctly. StoreFileVersion::replace() exists at app/Actions/Files/StoreFileVersion.php:132, built by b7836ef under item/api-presigned-upload, with no File::create() in its body and no name argument for a caller to pass wrong; tests/Feature/StoreFileVersionReplaceTest.php covers it. The 2026-09-20 comment on that issue said the work was unbuilt and needed an owner-created item -- it was already present in the very commit that comment cites as the state it was read against, checked with git log -S and git show rather than recalled. A correction is posted on the issue.\n\n#109 WAS CLOSED IN ERROR AND REOPENED WITHIN THE HOUR. This is the pass's own defect and it is recorded as one. moveFile() and moveDirectory() were read, found scoped to viewableDirectoryIds() inside the query, and the whole issue was concluded discharged. It was not: selectDirectory() (Browser.php:270-291) holds the opposite posture on purpose and its comment names #109 as where the question belongs. Three call sites, two read, the product concluded from the two -- CLAUDE.md's 'counting readers is not counting sources' with call sites as the readers. The issue's own 2026-09-20 comment sets out both positions and says they cannot both be adopted, and it was not read before the close.\n\nTHE CLOSING EDIT ALSO OVERWROTE THE ISSUE BODY, which is the worse half. GitHub keeps prior revisions and they are reachable from the body's pencil menu, but no GraphQL tool is available in this environment to pull userContentEdits, so the body was reconstructed from the code and from that comment, labelled as a reconstruction, and readers are pointed at the edit history in preference to it. The two parts the comment identifies as stale -- a patch naming entry points that no longer exist in that shape, and a paragraph decision/0093 falsified -- were deliberately not reinstated as live claims.\n\ndecision/0109 records the rule both halves point at: folding an issue into a clause records where the work will be done and does not narrow what the issue asks, so an item completing means re-reading each folded issue against its OWN text and comments. LOOP.md step 1 changes in the same commit, per step 7. And item/api-content's doneWhen is amended: its claim that the posture was 'settled ONCE for the whole product' is false, and it is the sentence that made the wrong closure look supported.\n\nNO CODE CHANGED IN THIS PASS. The v1 gap is still six and the five owner decisions are unchanged.\n\nA CONSULTATION WAS RUN, overdue by well more than LOOP.md step 8's four iterations, and it found that the loop had been declining work on a premise it made up. The headline is not the consultation's advice; it is what verifying the advice turned over.\n\nTHE INVENTED PREMISE. decision/0107 says \"creating items is the owner's under decision/0105\". 0105 says no such thing -- its only statement about the owner is that a SCHEMA change is theirs. The phrase occurs exactly once in the ledger, in the decision that coined it, and it was used there to hold a restriction against an earlier consultation, then repeated a day later to call issue #109 the owner's. decision/0110 retracts it, keeps decision/0102's restriction on its own surfaces (which stands on 0102's own reasoning), and concludes #109 is the loop's: it is a defect against a clause the loop itself wrote and merged.\n\nA FALSE CITATION IN THREE PLACES. LOOP.md step 4, item/docs-site and item/readme-owner all attribute to decision/0099 the error \"reading 'this item ends at something I cannot authorise' as 'nothing in this item can be built'\". 0099 is about a branch-gated job being invisible to the PR whose merge it will break, and its rationale contains neither word. Corrected in all three, in the same commit as the decision, per LOOP.md step 7.\n\nA CYCLE IN THE ROAD TO v1, verified rather than taken on the consultation's word. item/readme-owner clause 2 wants the quick start to use the published image; README.md:10 pulls with no tag, so it resolves `latest`; release.yml publishes `latest` only on a non-pre-release tag; that tag is item/tag-v1-0-0, which dependsOn item/v1-audit, which dependsOn item/readme-owner. The dependsOn graph is acyclic and the clause semantics are not. The rc does not break it and leaves verify-latest-resolves-to-the-tag unexecuted until v1.0.0 -- the one tag that cannot be retried. Recorded in the clause; the way out (cut a real v0.1.0) is a release decision and is filed as an issue.\n\nWHERE THE CONSULTATION WAS WRONG, checked before acting, as LOOP.md step 9 requires. It said the Livewire side holds 403 in \"exactly two tests\" at FileBrowserActionsTest:75 and FileBrowserTest:88. Neither line number is an assertForbidden() call (they are :84 and :91), and non-API assertForbidden() appears about forty times across eighteen files. Most concern permission level rather than reach, so the narrow claim may yet hold, but it is not established and the restated clause decision/0110 requires will have to count them properly. Right about substance, wrong about details, for the third consultation running.\n\nTHE FIRST ITEM THE LOOP HAS CREATED SINCE decision/0110 RETRACTED THE PREMISE AGAINST IT. item/reach-existence-oracle (order 75, v1.0.0, M, Active) restates issue #109 before any code moves, which is decision/0068's requirement and the reason this is a ledger pass rather than a code one.\n\nTHE RESTATEMENT IS THE SUBSTANCE, not a formality. #109 has asked '404 or 403' for four days and that framing is wrong. The security property is whether a surface hands the caller an EXISTENCE ORACLE -- whether a subject that exists outside the viewer's reach answers DIFFERENTLY from an id that does not exist. Which status the two share is local; that they agree is the requirement. This was established from the code: FileVersionDownloadController closes the oracle at 403 and argues it in its own test ('there is no existence oracle here'), while the movers close it at 404 by scoping. Both are coherent. What leaks is `findOrFail()` then `authorize()`, which answers 404 for a nonexistent id and 403 for an unreachable one -- and that shape is live at six sites in Browser.php: dropMove() destination 587, its two subjects 590 and 604, trashFileRow() 431, preview() 476, selectDirectory() 270-291.\n\nCLAUSE 1 CAN LAND ALONE. dropMove()'s destination is an unswept MOVE DESTINATION, and the product already settled that those answer 404 -- so it needs no posture decision at all. The comment on #109 from 2026-09-20 named line 587 and it was read as a stale-patch correction rather than a missed call site.\n\nTHE TEST SET WAS COUNTED BY READING. Nine tests hold the reach answers; non-API assertForbidden() appears about forty times across eighteen files and the rest are permission LEVEL. Both earlier counts were wrong: this loop said three call sites, and the consultation said 'exactly two tests' at line numbers that are not assertForbidden() calls.\n\nTHE v1 GAP GOES FROM SIX TO SEVEN, said plainly per decision/0105 rather than buried. 132ae3d recorded.\n\nCODE, FOR THE FIRST TIME IN THIS RUN'S LATER HALF. item/reach-existence-oracle clause 1 shipped in PR #305: dropMove()'s destination scoped to viewableDirectoryIds() inside the query, with a TEST PAIR because one case cannot show that two answers agree. Recorded as mutation/0038, proven both directions in the guards job.\n\nAND THE FINDING THAT OUTRANKS THE FIX. guards went red on the first push -- not on the new entry, which passed, but on a-dropped-row-is-re-authorised-at-both-ends, a pre-existing guard the diff never mentioned. That entry removes dropMove()'s authorize('move', ...). Its witness was the out-of-reach test, which exercised the authorize only because an out-of-reach destination used to be resolved by a bare findOrFail() and refused downstream. Scoping moved the refusal earlier, so deleting the authorize stopped changing that test's outcome. The authorisation never weakened; the proof of it did. decision/0111 records the class -- CLAUDE.md's existing rule covers a test that NEVER exercised its guard, this is one that DID and then stopped -- and CLAUDE.md gains the rule in the same commit per step 7.\n\nWHAT THIS SAYS ABOUT EVIDENCE, sharper than mutation/0037's version. That node warned a GREEN guards job does not prove a given entry ran. This run supplies the converse: a RED guards job does not mean YOUR entry failed. Same remedy either way -- read the entry's two lines out of the log rather than the job's colour.\n\nTHE ITEM STAYS ACTIVE ON PURPOSE. Clause 2's five sites and clause 3's false preview() docblock are untouched. Marking it Completed on one clause would be decision/0087's substitution. The v1 gap is SEVEN, unchanged by this pass: the item it counts was created this morning and has not closed.\n\nb375332 and 443f830 recorded.\n\nTHE ORACLE IS CLOSED ACROSS Browser.php, in PRs #305, #307 and #308 -- seven sites, each with a test pair and a registered mutation proven both directions, the guards job reporting 99 then 100 load-bearing and read entry by entry rather than by colour.\n\nAND THE ENUMERATION THAT DIRECTED IT WAS WRONG FOUR TIMES, which decision/0112 records as the pass's real finding. Two sites it listed were not sites (selectDirectory()'s nested branch was already closed; PropertyPanel has no lookup). A whole surface was missing (four route-model-bound subjects). And the claim that FileVersionDownloadController \"closes the oracle\" was true of its version id and false of its file id -- a half-truth that had been load-bearing, since it was one of the three positions the reframing rested on. Every one came from inferring which code a test exercises from its NAME rather than its CALL.\n\nTHE BINDING SURFACES BECOME THEIR OWN ITEM, item/reach-oracle-route-binding, and the v1 gap goes from SEVEN to EIGHT. Stated here and in the item, per decision/0105: the loop will not create an item and report the gap unchanged.\n\n44919e0 (#308) IS NOT RECORDED IN THIS PASS. Its tests run on main was still in progress when this was written, and a merge entry carries a conclusion -- recording one unread is the shape of defect CLAUDE.md's citation rule names. It is the trailing gap the validator permits and rides the next pass.\n\nAND THE VALIDATOR CAUGHT THIS PASS SKIPPING A MERGE, which is worth recording rather than quietly fixing. a7d98cc (#306) was omitted while d55a529 was recorded, making it an INTERIOR gap -- \"a merge nobody wrote down, not one not yet recorded\", in the checker's own words. That is precisely the distinction item/ledger-main-push-record was built to draw, and the rule fired on its author in the same pass that praised reading evidence rather than assuming it. Recorded in sequence. The second failure was smaller and the same family: the item's `result` named PR #308 before any PullRequest node for it existed, so a reference pointed at nothing.\n\nitem/reach-existence-oracle IS COMPLETED. Every site it enumerates answers identically for a subject outside the viewer's reach and for an id that does not exist, each proven by a test pair and a registered mutation (0038, 0039-0043) that the guards job re-runs on every push. Its boundary is Browser.php; the route-model-binding surfaces are a separate item and were never in scope.\n\nTHE GAP, ALL THREE NUMBERS, because the net would hide both moves (decision/0105). It stood at SEVEN this morning. Creating item/reach-oracle-route-binding took it to EIGHT. Completing this item takes it back to SEVEN. The day's code work closed an information disclosure across seven call sites and left the v1 gap exactly where it started -- which is the honest reading, not a disappointing one: the item did not exist at 06:44 either.\n\nRECORDED IN DEPENDENCY ORDER, because the validator enforces it and caught the previous pass getting it wrong: PR #308's node first, then mutation/0043 which points at it, then #308 back into the item's `result`, then the merge entry. b652592 (#309) was held when the preceding paragraph was written -- ledger 569 and pages 63 had concluded success, tests had not, and a merge entry carries a conclusion. tests 573 has since concluded success, so it is RECORDED HERE rather than deferred to another pass: the reason for holding it was that its conclusion was unread, and that reason expired before this branch left the workstation. pullRequest is null because #309 is a ledger-branch PR, the same case as #292, #297, #298 and #306. The trailing gap is now this branch's own merge, which no pass can record before it exists.\n\nTHE PASS THAT MARKED item/reach-existence-oracle COMPLETED CITED A LINE THAT WAS NEVER PRINTED, and that is this pass's subject rather than a footnote. Its first commit message quoted \"Compared 19 commits\" from validate-ledger.php. No such line was emitted. MAIN_SHA is a POSITIONAL argument and `MAIN_SHA=<sha> php validate-ledger.php` sets a variable the script never reads, so $mainSha stayed null, the entire main-push-history check was skipped, and the output was the same \"Ledger is sound\" a full run prints. Three consecutive local invocations took that form. The number 19 was not read anywhere; it was inferred from the merge just added.\n\nTHE TRUE FIGURE IS 18, and it was established twice from independent origins rather than twice from the same one, which is decision/0080's other half. The correct positional invocation reports \"Compared 18 commits on main since aeccb306\"; `git rev-list --first-parent --count aeccb306..b652592` independently answers 18; and run/0021 held 18 merge entries. Corrected in 3673a52 before the branch shipped.\n\ndecision/0113 RECORDS THE CLASS, and it is not decision/0080 again. That rule, and decision/0112's extension of it to enumerations, are about the READER's discipline in checking a citation. This is about the TOOL's. validate-ledger.php's own comment at line 209 had ANTICIPATED this exact failure -- it says in as many words that \"checked twelve and found no hole\" and \"checked nothing\" otherwise print the same cheerful \"Ledger is sound\", and cites the job that passed for its entire life while validating nothing -- and then chose, as its defence, to print a count. A defence that defends by printing needs the reader to notice an ABSENT line, which is a non-event indistinguishable from not having looked, and it is defeated outright by the `| tail -4` these runs are actually invoked with, because the verdict is at the bottom and the scope line is at the top. A guard delegated to a reader is not a guard. CLAUDE.md gains the rule in the same commit, per LOOP.md step 7.\n\nFIXED RATHER THAN LEARNED FROM, as issue #311 and PR #312. The env-var-with-no-positional invocation now exits 1 naming the mistake; a run with no sha by any route now says \"the main-push-history check did NOT run\" on STDERR, where before it said nothing whatsoever. A new CI job asserts both, with a POSITIVE CONTROL, without which two refusal assertions would be satisfied by a script that refused everything. Its log was read in full rather than by colour -- reading the tail of that particular log would have been the same mistake a third time.\n\nTHE MUTATION WAS RUN BY HAND BECAUSE THE WITNESS IS A CI JOB, and only the FIRST step flips. With the condition replaced by `if (false)` the load-bearing step exits 0 -- \"Ledger is sound\" off an invocation that checked nothing, which is the defect itself -- and restored it exits 1 with \"POSITIONAL\" in the output. The second step does NOT flip: the \"did NOT run\" notice prints either way. That is said because CLAUDE.md already records that reasoning about which half of a check carries the weight is unreliable, and here the mutation settled it rather than an argument.\n\n#311 STAYS OPEN AND NO ITEM WAS CREATED FOR THE REMAINING HALF, which is a judgement and is recorded as one so the next reader can overturn it. mutations.json's expectFailing names a Pest test and the sweep runs Pest, so registering this guard needs a test that subprocesses PHP -- CLAUDE.md's \"no test may require an external binary\" read from an angle it was not written for, with no precedent anywhere in tests/. That is real work and it is tracked by the open issue. It was NOT made a ledger item because every ledger-tooling item in this ledger carries release v1.0.0 (item/ledger-rule-witnesses, item/ledger-main-push-record and item/mutation-filter-regex all do), so creating one would take the v1 gap from SEVEN to EIGHT for a guard that is proven load-bearing today and blocks no v1 surface. The cost of the judgement is stated plainly: nothing re-proves that guard automatically, and if it rots, nothing will say so.\n\n#312 CARRIES pullRequest: null AND IS THE THIRD CONCRETE INSTANCE OF decision/0102's ACCOUNTING QUESTION. It is not a ledger-branch PR -- it changes .github/scripts/ and .github/workflows/ -- but a PullRequest node requires a non-empty `implements`, and no item names this surface. #293 was the first such case (CHANGELOG.md, on the release critical path) and the reasoning there applies unchanged. Recorded rather than worked around: a merged PR that fixes a real defect in the ledger's own checker is a row the ledger cannot name.\n\nda44214 (#312) IS RECORDED HERE, not held. Its tests run 579 was still in progress when the paragraphs above were written, and it concluded success before this branch was pushed -- tests 579, ledger 575, pages 69, all success at run_attempt 1, read per check name. The rule is that a merge entry carries a conclusion, not that a merge waits a pass; the moment the conclusion is read the entry is owed. Applied twice in a row now, to b652592 and to this one, which is the difference between a trailing gap being a state and it becoming a habit.\n\nTHE v1 GAP IS SEVEN, unchanged by this pass. No item moved, none was created, and neither PR #312 nor this one touches a v1 item's clauses.\n\nTHE ENUMERATION WAS WRONG AGAIN, IN THE ITEM decision/0112 CREATED, within a day of creating it. item/reach-oracle-route-binding said FOUR route-model-bound sites. There are FIVE. DirectoryArchiveDownloadController::__invoke(DirectoryArchive $archive) carries the same oracle: a nonexistent archive id 404s from the binding, one belonging to another user is bound and answers 403 from its requested_by check. Confirmed from the test rather than reasoned about -- DirectoryArchiveTest asserts assertForbidden() for that case -- and the model carries no global scope, its booted() only assigning a uuid.\n\ndecision/0114 RECORDS WHAT ACTUALLY FIXES THIS, because decision/0112's remedy was \"check it harder\" and this is what \"check it harder\" produced one day later. The difference between the census that missed the site and the one that found it is not care, it is FORM. The first listed places someone had looked, whose completeness is a claim about the census-taker's attention and which no reader has a lever against. The second asked a question the code answers mechanically -- which route parameters are resolved by implicit model binding at all -- and Laravel substitutes exactly when a parameter's type-hint is a Model, so one grep over controller and mount() signatures IS the set of candidate sites. An enumeration stated as a predicate can be re-run; one stated as a list can only be re-trusted.\n\nTHE SAME PREDICATE SETTLED THE FIX AND KILLED A CANDIDATE. Because every /api/v1 route declares `int $file` rather than a Model, implicit binding provably never runs there: the API is not a site, and it needs no protecting from a change made here. It also disqualifies Route::bind() outright -- that registers a binder keyed on parameter NAME, not type, so a global 'file' binder would hand a File model to every `int $file` API signature and TypeError the whole API. Both facts fell out of one question; a census by attention would have had to reason about each separately and could have been wrong about either.\n\nAND THE FIFTH SITE'S FIX WILL DISARM A REGISTERED GUARD, which is decision/0111 and is written into the item now rather than discovered mid-PR. `a-directory-archive-reaches-only-its-requester` removes exactly the abort_unless line that scoping would make unreachable. Unlike decision/0111's case there is no other test that still reaches it -- scoping makes the line dead, not merely unwitnessed -- so the entry is to be REPLACED by one on the scoping, its `why` naming the entry it succeeds. The protection and its proof move together; neither weakens.\n\nNO CODE IN THIS PASS, deliberately. decision/0068 asks for the restatement before the build, and the enumeration was wrong, so the restatement is the work. ee96e23 recorded, verified per check name first (tests 581, ledger 577, pages 71, all success at run_attempt 1).\n\nTHE v1 GAP IS STILL SEVEN. The item grew by a site; it did not split, and no item was created or completed.\n\nitem/reach-oracle-route-binding IS COMPLETED, and all FIVE of its sites are closed -- the four it originally named plus the one decision/0114's predicate found. Every route-model-bound subject now answers identically for a subject outside the viewer's reach and for an id that does not exist, each with a test pair and a registered mutation the guards job re-runs on every push (0044-0048).\n\nTHE GAP, ALL THREE NUMBERS (decision/0105). It was SEVEN at the start of this stretch. It stayed SEVEN when the item grew from four sites to five, because growing an item is not creating one. Completing it takes it to SIX. This is the first time in this run that closing an information-disclosure item actually moved the number, and it is worth saying why: its sibling item/reach-existence-oracle netted to zero only because it was created and completed inside the same day.\n\nTWO SHAPES, ONE PROPERTY, and the asymmetry is deliberate rather than untidy. Three File controllers stopped using implicit binding altogether -- `int $file` resolved through a scoped viewableFileOrFail() -- which is the shape the /api/v1 controllers already used and the shape FileVersionDownloadController had already adopted for its {version} parameter, arguing in its own docblock that binding an id before authorising it is an existence oracle and saying \"do not reintroduce it here\" while leaving {file} bound. Browser::mount() and DirectoryArchiveDownloadController keep their bound model and take a reach check instead: for the Browser, because ?int would change a signature ~105 Livewire::test() call sites pass a MODEL to, for no observable gain -- the caller sees a status and both cases answer 404 either way.\n\nRoute::bind() WAS THE OBVIOUS FIX AND WAS WRONG, established before a line was written rather than after a red CI. It is keyed on parameter NAME, not type, so a global 'file' binder would substitute a File model into every `int $file` signature in /api/v1 and TypeError the whole API. The same predicate that ruled it out proved the API is not a site at all.\n\nA GUARD WAS REPLACED RATHER THAN WEAKENED. Scoping the archive lookup to requested_by makes its old abort_unless UNREACHABLE -- not merely unwitnessed, which is decision/0111's case, but dead: nothing the query returns can fail it. decision/0111 says to repoint an entry at a test that still reaches its guard; where no such test can exist, the guard goes and the entry is replaced. mutation/0048 names the entry it succeeds and why.\n\nCI CAUGHT TWO THINGS AND BOTH WERE WORTH THE CYCLE.\n\nFirst, guards went red on PR #315 -- not on any new entry, all three of which reported \"fails, as it must\", but on the pre-existing file-version-download-cross-file-404, whose `remove` text the rename of $file to $model left matching 0 times. A RENAME CAN SILENTLY UNREGISTER A GUARD: decision/0111's family by another route, where the entry's TEXT stops finding the guard rather than its WITNESS stopping reaching it. Nothing silent happened, because mutation-check REFUSES a remove that does not match exactly once -- the shape decision/0113 asks for, and the exact contrast with the printed count that failed.\n\nSecond, PR #316's first push failed one test in all six matrix jobs with 1015 passing: FileBrowserTest's \"refuses to open a directory the viewer cannot see\" expected 403 from mount and got 404. That is the change working; the test encoded the old status.\n\ndecision/0114 CAUGHT ITS AUTHOR TWICE INSIDE ONE ITEM, which is the pass's real finding and is recorded as a cost rather than a vindication. Both misses have the same shape: I checked the things I had thought of instead of asking the question the code answers. For mutations I verified my three NEW entries matched once, where the mechanical question is whether EVERY entry's remove still matches once -- 104 entries, 0 mismatches, no Pest required, runnable here. For tests I grepped for a shape I guessed at, where the question is which tests expect a 403 from mount ITSELF, expressible as test(Browser::class, ...) then assertForbidden with no ->call( or ->set( between, since anything after a call is an ACTION refusal and untouched. Both now report clean, and both would have reported before the push. A decision about method is not learned by writing it down.\n\nAND A FABRICATED SHA WAS CAUGHT IN THIS PASS, by its author, before it was pushed. PR #316's node was first written with headSha d3eaf07c1d4c2d5b2f0a6b4c8e7a1f3d9b5c2e84 -- the real short sha d3eaf07 with thirty-three invented characters after it. It came from having the short form to hand and needing the long one. That is decision/0080's defect in its purest form: a value that looks like evidence and was never read. Every sha in every node this pass adds is now checked to resolve to a real commit with git cat-file, which is the decision/0114 form of the same discipline -- not \"is the one I am unsure about right\" but \"does every sha here exist\".\n\nTHE VALIDATOR THEN CAUGHT THE ORDERING, from a direction the earlier passes had not hit: PR #316's node names a mergeSha whose merge entry is not recorded, because 2867ebf's tests run had not concluded. Dependency order is not only \"a reference before its node\" -- it is also \"a PR node before the merge it claims\".\n\nALL THREE MERGES RECORDED, each verified per check name before its entry was written: 11e5026 (tests 583 / ledger 579 / pages 73), 41cc82b (tests 586 / ledger 582 / pages 76) and 2867ebf (tests 589 / ledger 585 / pages 79), all success at run_attempt 1, none cancelled.\n\n2867ebf was held while the paragraphs above were written and released the moment run 589 reported -- the third pass running to apply that rule, and the reason the trailing gap keeps closing within the pass that opens it rather than becoming a standing debt. The trailing gap is now only this branch's own merge, which no pass can record before it exists.",
